## 4. Workflow — spec-driven development loop

1. **Pick the next story.** Take the next unbuilt `STORY-0NN` from `docs/stories/Aaradhya_Story_Backlog.md`, in the order the backlog already sequences them — don't jump ahead to a later story whose dependencies (an earlier schema, an earlier endpoint) aren't built yet.
2. **Open a session and prompt with the story, not a paraphrase of it.** Paste the story's full block — title, flow, acceptance criteria, UI needed, tokens, edge cases — verbatim, plus the path to the relevant `CLAUDE.md` if Claude Code hasn't already picked it up from the working directory. Don't summarize the story from memory; the acceptance criteria are the actual spec for this unit of work, and paraphrasing is where scope drifts.
3. **Build.** Claude Code implements against the story's acceptance criteria only. The root `CLAUDE.md` already tells it to stop and ask rather than expand scope if the story and the spec seem to disagree — if that happens, resolve it before continuing, don't let the session guess.
4. **Test against the story's own acceptance criteria checklist.** Each unchecked box is a concrete test — write/run the test that proves it, not a vague smoke check. A story isn't done until every box in its Acceptance Criteria is genuinely verifiable, not just "looks right."
5. **Run `review-standards` against the diff.** `/review-standards HEAD~1` (or whatever fixed point marks the start of this story's work) before committing — catches naming, layering, and smell issues while the story is still fresh, not at PR review time.
6. **Commit, referencing the story ID.** Format stays exactly what `docs/git-guidelines.md` already specifies (action verb first, specific, under 120 characters) with the story ID appended:
   `Add accommodation totals computation (STORY-018)`
   Not `STORY-018: add accommodation totals` — the verb-first rule from git-guidelines.md still governs; the story ID is metadata appended, not a prefix that pushes the verb out of first position.
7. **Open the PR titled with the story ID, and paste the story's Acceptance Criteria as the PR's checklist.** `[STORY-018] Accommodation totals computation` as the title; the body's test-plan section is literally that story's checklist from the backlog, checked off against what was actually verified in step 4 — so the PR is reviewable against the same objective bar the story was written against, not a re-derived one.
8. **Merge, then move to the next story.** Don't batch multiple stories into one PR even when they feel related (e.g. STORY-018/019/020's schema→endpoint→UI chain for Accommodation) — each was deliberately scoped small enough to review and test alone; merging them separately is what keeps that true in practice, not just on paper.

